<h2>A death that began at a gutka shop</h2>
<p>The occurrence was brief and public. On 27 November 2017, near ISBT Anand Vihar, the accused quarrelled with a young man after buying gutka. He picked up a wooden danda lying at a temporary barricade and struck the victim on the head; after the victim fell, he struck once more. Bystanders caught him on the spot. The victim was declared brought dead, and the post-mortem attributed death to cranio-cerebral damage caused by blunt force. The defence version — that the victim, intoxicated, fell on his own — was rejected at trial along with the solitary defence eyewitness, and the trial court convicted under Section 302 IPC, imposing life imprisonment and a fine of Rs 50,000.</p>

<h2>The appellate analysis</h2>
<p>The Division Bench did not disturb the finding that the appellant caused the fatal injuries; the appeal turned entirely on classification. Walking through the Exception 4 ingredients, the Court found no prior enmity and no premeditation — the parties were strangers brought together by a petty quarrel minutes earlier. The weapon was not carried to the scene but picked up from a barricade, the classic weapon of opportunity. And the two blows fell in quick succession, in a single transaction of passion, with no interval that could mark a considered decision to finish the victim.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Trial court</h3>
    <p>Second blow after the victim fell treated as cruel and unusual conduct, excluding Exception 4; conviction under Section 302 IPC with life imprisonment.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>High Court</h3>
    <p>Blows in quick succession within one sudden transaction; no undue advantage, following Sukhbir Singh v. State of Haryana; conviction altered to Section 304 Part II, seven years RI and fine of Rs 50,000, default simple imprisonment of one and a half years.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Custody arithmetic</h2>
<p>The Court noted that as of 30 June 2026, the appellant had served about five years, ten months and fourteen days, with nine months of earned remission in addition. Against a substituted term of seven years, the practical consequence is that the appellant approaches the tail end of his sentence, with the remission system left to operate on the recalculated term.</p>

<h2>The wider pattern</h2>
<p>Street altercations that end in a single fatal escalation are among the most frequently re-classified cases in Delhi criminal appeals. The controlling questions are always the same: who brought the weapon, how much time separated quarrel from blow, and whether the assault continued after the deceased was helpless. Prosecutors seeking to hold a Section 302 conviction must show something that converts passion into design — a fetched weapon, a pause, a targeted vital-organ attack pressed home. Defence counsel, conversely, should build the Exception 4 record at trial itself: the spontaneity of the quarrel, the provenance of the weapon and the continuity of the transaction are facts, and they are proved or lost in cross-examination long before the appeal is argued.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Exception 4 does not reward violence; it calibrates culpability. A seven-year term with fine remains a severe sentence. Families of victims in such cases retain the remedy of victim compensation under the DLSA schemes, which operates independently of the classification of the offence.</p>
</div>
